I. Adding the meta-productxy layer to your build
=================================================

Run 'bitbake-layers add-layer meta-productxy'

II. Misc
========

On Verdin iMX8MP with the Mallow device tree, the device-tree-overlays recipe
builds and enables verdin-imx8mp_spi1-gpio_overlay.dtbo. The u-boot-distro-boot
configuration also loads it after the existing HMP overlay.

Despite the retained spi1-gpio filename, this overlay now releases SPI_1
(ecspi1) for the M7, not Linux GPIO use. It disables the Linux SPI controller,
including spidev and the Mallow TPM, and does not apply pin mux settings.
The M7 firmware must configure ECSPI1 clocks, pad mux/control and chip selects:

  SODIMM 196: GPIO5_IO06 (SPI_1 clock)
  SODIMM 200: GPIO5_IO07 (SPI_1 MOSI)
  SODIMM 198: GPIO5_IO08 (SPI_1 MISO)
  SODIMM 202: GPIO5_IO09 (SPI_1 chip select 0)
  SODIMM  64: GPIO3_IO16 (Mallow TPM chip select 1)

Use the ECSPI1 alternate functions for clock, MOSI and MISO. SODIMM 202 can
use GPIO5_IO09 or ECSPI1_SS0, depending on the M7 driver. SODIMM 64 is the
TPM chip-select connection in the Mallow design; keep it inactive (high)
when addressing another SPI device if the TPM is populated.

Linux GPIO controllers remain enabled because other board functions use
them. This overlay does not enforce exclusive M7 access to individual GPIO
lines: do not use gpioset/gpioget or bind Linux drivers to these five pins.
Do not reset an entire shared GPIO bank from M7 firmware.

Build in the configured Yocto environment:

  bitbake device-tree-overlays u-boot-distro-boot
  bitbake torizon-docker

Deploy the rebuilt image and reboot. Verify with:

  gpioinfo | grep -E 'SODIMM_(196|200|198|202|64)"'

The lines should have no Linux consumer. /dev/spidev1.0 should no longer
exist. Verify SPI transfers using the M7 firmware and a logic analyzer;
the Linux overlay alone does not initialize or test M7 SPI operation.

Do not enable another overlay that re-enables ecspi1 after this overlay,
with one intended exception: verdin-imx8mp_icm45686_overlay.dtbo (see below)
deliberately takes SPI_1 back for Linux. When that overlay is active, the
M7 firmware must not drive the SPI_1 pins.

SHT40 sensors
=============

verdin-imx8mp_sht40_overlay.dtbo adds two Sensirion SHT40 temperature and
humidity sensors at 7-bit addresses 0x44 and 0x45 on Verdin I2C_1 (&i2c4,
Linux i2c-3). SDA is Mallow X10-10 / SODIMM 12; SCL is X10-11 / SODIMM 14.
These X10 signals use 1.8 V logic. Use compatible supply and pull-up voltages.
The sensors need different factory-fixed address variants.

The overlay is enabled in both the TEZI boot overlay list and uEnv.txt.
sht4x.cfg selects the hwmon driver as a module, and torizon-docker includes
kernel-module-sht4x. After rebuilding and deploying the image, readings are
available at:

  /sys/bus/i2c/devices/3-0044/hwmon/hwmon*/temp1_input
  /sys/bus/i2c/devices/3-0044/hwmon/hwmon*/humidity1_input
  /sys/bus/i2c/devices/3-0045/hwmon/hwmon*/temp1_input
  /sys/bus/i2c/devices/3-0045/hwmon/hwmon*/humidity1_input

Temperature is in millidegrees Celsius; humidity is in milli-percent RH.
The driver caches measurements for at least two seconds. Do not rely on
fixed hwmon indices or access the sensors with raw I2C commands while bound.

ICM-45686 SPI driver
====================

The Verdin iMX8MP linux-toradex kernel backports the upstream Linux v6.19
ICM-45600 IIO driver, including ICM-45686 support. icm45600.cfg enables
the SPI transport as a module; the I2C and I3C transports remain disabled.
torizon-docker includes kernel-module-inv-icm45600-spi and its dependencies.

verdin-imx8mp_icm45686_overlay.dtbo instantiates the sensor on Verdin
SPI_1 (ecspi1) chip select 0, with INT1 on SODIMM_206:

  SPI_1_CLK   X19-1   SODIMM_196  ECSPI1_SCLK
  SPI_1_MISO  X19-2   SODIMM_198  ECSPI1_MISO
  SPI_1_MOSI  X19-3   SODIMM_200  ECSPI1_MOSI
  SPI_1_CS    X19-4   SODIMM_202  ECSPI1 chip select 0
  INT1        X10-21  SODIMM_206  GPIO1_IO00 ("conversion finished")
  INT2        X10-18  SODIMM_15   GPIO5_IO05 (described, unused by driver)

SODIMM_15 is Verdin PWM_1; the overlay disables pwm1 so the pad becomes
an input and cannot drive against the sensor's INT2 output.

These header signals use 1.8V logic: the sensor VDDIO must be supplied
with 1.8V, and VDD within the sensor's 1.71-3.6V range. The overlay's
vdd-supply/vddio-supply reference the always-on +V3.3_SW and +V1.8_SW
carrier rails only to satisfy the binding; wire the breakout accordingly.

The overlay re-enables ecspi1 for Linux, so it supersedes the SPI_1 M7
handoff of verdin-imx8mp_spi1-gpio_overlay.dtbo and must be listed after
it (as done in uEnv.txt and the TEZI boot overlay list). The M7 firmware
must not drive the SPI_1 pins while this overlay is active. The Mallow
TPM on chip select 1 becomes visible again.

Reading the ICM-45686
=====================

After rebuilding and deploying torizon-docker, verify module and probe:

  modinfo inv-icm45600-spi
  dmesg | grep -i icm45
  grep . /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:device*/name

The driver registers two IIO devices, icm45686-accel and icm45686-gyro.
Below, $A and $G are their /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceN directories.

One-shot readings (sysfs, polled):

  cat $A/in_accel_x_raw $A/in_accel_y_raw $A/in_accel_z_raw
  cat $A/in_accel_scale            # acceleration = raw * scale [m/s^2]
  cat $G/in_anglvel_x_raw $G/in_anglvel_y_raw $G/in_anglvel_z_raw
  cat $G/in_anglvel_scale          # rate = raw * scale [rad/s]
  cat $A/in_temp_raw $A/in_temp_offset $A/in_temp_scale
                                   # T = (raw + offset) * scale [m°C]

Sampling rate (per device, in Hz):

  cat $A/sampling_frequency_available
  echo 100 > $A/sampling_frequency

Buffered capture via FIFO and INT1 (example: accelerometer X/Y/Z with
timestamps, 32 samples):

  echo 1 > $A/scan_elements/in_accel_x_en
  echo 1 > $A/scan_elements/in_accel_y_en
  echo 1 > $A/scan_elements/in_accel_z_en
  echo 1 > $A/scan_elements/in_timestamp_en
  echo 32 > $A/buffer/length
  echo 1 > $A/buffer/enable
  cat /dev/iio:deviceN | xxd | head    # binary records per scan_elements
  echo 0 > $A/buffer/enable

Record layout follows the in_*_type and in_*_index attributes in
scan_elements (little-endian 16-bit samples plus a 64-bit timestamp).
Tools such as iio_info/iio_readdev (libiio, not in the image) or the
kernel's iio_generic_buffer can decode the stream.

Do not keep the sysfs raw attributes and the buffer in use at the same
time; raw reads return -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.
